meta.gold_solution:
Operational problem; parity.
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline & \multicolumn{2}{|c|}{ Ball drawing situation } & \multicolumn{4}{|c|}{ Changes in the number of red, blue, and green balls } \\
\hline & Balls taken out & Ball(s) put back & Blue balls & Red balls & Green balls \\
\hline (1) & 1 blue ball, 1 green ball & 1 red ball & -1 & +1 & -1 \\
\hline (2) & 1 red ball, 1 green ball & 1 red ball & unchanged & unchanged & -1 \\
\hline (3) & 2 red balls & 2 blue balls & +2 & -2 & unchanged \\
\hline \multirow{3}{*}{ (4) } & 2 blue balls & 1 green ball & -2 & unchanged & +1 \\
\cline { 1 - 6 } & 2 green balls & 1 green ball & unchanged & unchanged & -1 \\
\cline { 1 - 6 } & 1 blue ball, 1 red ball & 1 green ball & -1 & -1 & +1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

From the table, we can observe that the parity of the number of red balls and blue balls changes simultaneously;
and initially, there are 1001 blue balls and 1000 red balls in the bottle;
that is, initially, there are an odd number of blue balls and an even number of red balls, and the parity of the number of blue balls and red balls is different; thus, no matter how the operations are performed, the parity of the number of blue balls and red balls will always be different;
Therefore, it is impossible to end up with "0 blue balls, 0 red balls, 1 green ball."
We also notice that in the situations of putting the balls back, there is no situation of "putting back 1 blue ball";
thus, it is also impossible to end up with "1 blue ball, 0 red balls, 0 green balls."
In summary, the remaining ball is a red ball.

